      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Precision limitation: the twelve historicalContext values are the official snapshots' published whole-percentage-point shares. The CMS raw CSV and API could not be retrieved through the web tool, so exact historical CMS numerators and one-decimal ratios were not fetched. No exact counts were reconstructed from rounded percentages. The forecast resolver still requires the actual first-published counts."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view reference class is twelve distinct California original monthly prints from July 2025 through June 2026. Their base rate has mean 41.4167% and median 43%; February-June 2026 also averages 43%. June's latest 44% observation supports modest upward shrinkage from the median. The earlier August and January declines argue against treating recent stability as the full uncertainty distribution."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Policy mechanism: DHCS ACWDL 25-14 reinstates asset verification at annual renewals for affected non-MAGI beneficiaries beginning January 2026. Additional information requirements could reduce automatic renewals, but this policy is already present in the recent observations. I apply no additional August policy adjustment. No fetched official source established that this policy caused January's decline or that August 2025's decline will recur seasonally."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Release-date verification limitation: the official CMS snapshot index and June report identify June 2026's publication as September 25, 2026. No official future release calendar or announcement confirming December 15, 2026 for August 2026 was fetched. The registered resolutionDate is retained, but it is unverified; the requirement to verify that date from an official schedule remains unsatisfied."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: use a shrinkage persistence model with a 43% reference-class median prior and twelve original monthly prints. Level adjustment = 0.5 percentage points from equally weighting the 43% prior and latest 44% print; momentum adjustment = 0; one-off adjustment = 0; incremental policy adjustment = 0. Point = 43.5%. The eleven successive changes are [0,10,2,-1,-1,-9,8,0,-1,1,1], with sum 10 and sum of squares 254. Sample sigma = 4.9488 percentage points, calculated as sqrt((254 - 10^2/11)/10). The one-month 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 6.3345 points. For the two-month June-to-August horizon, assume independent monthly innovations and add whole-point rounding variance 1/12: half-width = 1.28*sqrt(2*sigma^2 + 1/12) = 8.9660 points. Rounding 43.5 +/- 8.9660 to one decimal gives final implied bounds 34.5% and 52.5%. These are approximate model bounds because historical shares are rounded and changes include abrupt reversals."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Point estimate = 0.5*43 + 0.5*44 = 43.5%. The 80% interval is [round(43.5 - 8.9659547,1), round(43.5 + 8.9659547,1)] = [34.5%,52.5%]. Resolution uses round(100*Metric 5a1/Metric 5,1) from the first official August 2026 O row."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk: improved automatic data matching or an easier renewal cohort raises the ex parte share. Downside risk: a heavier verification cohort, more required forms or processing disruptions lowers it. A repeat of August 2025's published 34% would land below the interval; a substantial automation improvement pushing the share above 52.5% would land outside the interval on the upside. These are scenarios, not fetched August outcomes."
      },
